y' in SRC_003 is an abstraction of the specific 'critical attitude' described in SRC_004, generalizing the methodological choice to bet on high-content theories. Refuted If: 'Verisimilitude' cannot be defined without probabilistic assumptions, or if the 'critical attitude' itself relies on an inductive habit of mind, rendering the abstraction baseless.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"05e7096fadd9de5d7aabfc9b52de4f1726e6a9a642508e3717f9769dc2afada0"},{"role":"dependence","target":"4dfcde0c96c46f06c4ca5541463ed52b1212f826d2abb071a6fca11fb3227d9b"}],"typicality":0.65},{"content":"Relation Kind: Reduction. The dispute over whether preference smuggles induction (SRC_005) reduces to the single variable of whether 'acting on corroboration' necessarily assumes the future will resemble the past (SRC_001). Refuted If: One can justify action based solely on the elimination of error without predicting success, or if the assumption is merely a psychological habit rather than a logical premise.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"c82de43e800f8bbc17546a615b1105f004ddc362f858f8ec10422042fbb14ab3"},{"role":"dependence","target":"a15f4b263b9598e290bb50f8612ea5e31dd02988e16e61a29a969e35df8d58d4"}],"typicality":0.55},{"content":"Relation Kind: Compatibility. The defense of preference as a 'critical rationality' decision rule (SRC_003) is compatible with the accusation that it smuggles induction (SRC_002) only if the concept of 'rationality' allows for non-justificational decision rules that functionally rely on inductive habits. Refuted If: Popper can demonstrate a form of 'rational action' that requires zero expectation of future success, or if the preference implies an expectation that the future will resemble the past, making them mutually exclusive.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"05e7096fadd9de5d7aabfc9b52de4f1726e6a9a642508e3717f9769dc2afada0"},{"role":"dependence","target":"28c31d36f3430bfcde886ac08aadc